+A training procedure that alternately removes and re-samples particles to manage particle count and convergence behavior. The count of re-sampled particles gradually decreases to zero as the model converges (e.g., after 10K steps on D-NeRF dataset) [S17].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training time varies significantly by method and hardware. For example, 25k iterations on a single RTX 3090 may take 1-2 days for generalizable modules [S9], while per-scene optimization with voxel grids can take ~15 minutes [S2]. Dynamic scenes with 200k iterations may take hours to a day on 4x A5000 GPUs [S7].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on grid resolution and batch size. High-resolution grids (e.g., $512^3$) and large batch sizes (e.g., 8192 rays) require significant VRAM, often necessitating mixed precision or gradient checkpointing [S2, S10].
-*   **Scaling:** Performance generally improves with more iterations and larger batch sizes, but with diminishing returns and increased computational cost [S8, S15]. Upsampling strategies can improve quality but increase memory usage [S11][S12, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Flexible configuration allows tuning for specific datasets and hardware constraints. Mixed precision and CUDA acceleration significantly speed up training [S12, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Hyperparameter tuning is non-trivial and sensitive to dataset characteristics. Overfitting can occur with excessive iterations or insufficient regularization [S13, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Standard for training any differentiable radiance field. Specific configurations (e.g., warm-start, dynamic masking) are used for specialized tasks like dynamic scenes or few-shot learning.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training time varies significantly by method and hardware. Generalizable NeRF modules take about 2 days [S9]. Fine-tuning ranges from 30 to 90 minutes depending on video frames [S9]. Neural appearance refinement takes 1 to 2 days [S9]. Some methods complete per-scene optimization in about 15 minutes [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the representation. Hash grids (e.g., Zip-NeRF) use 20 levels with $2^{21}$ entries per table [S6]. Voxel grids may use $512^3$ dense grids [S10] or $190^3$ voxels for deformation [S8].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On a single NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada GPU, FPS measurements are taken [S5]. On 4x 24GB A5000 GPUs, training takes a few hours to one day [S7].
+*   **Scaling:** Coarse-to-fine strategies upsample voxel resolutions in xy-dimensions every 40k iterations [S12]. Progressive schemes train highest LOD first, then add levels every 100 epochs [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Allows fine-grained control over convergence behavior. Supports various regularization techniques and optimization algorithms.
+*   **Limitations:** Hyperparameter sensitivity can be high. Some configurations (e.g., large batch sizes) require significant memory.
+*   **When to Use:** Standard for training NeRF variants. Essential for reproducing results from published papers.
+*   **When Not to Use:** Not applicable for inference-only scenarios or pre-trained model usage.
 
 ## Background
-The concept of training configuration in neural radiance fields emerged with the original NeRF paper [S19, S10] and has evolved alongside various representations (MLP, Voxel, Hash Grids). Key developments include the introduction of multi-resolution hash encoding [S6], voxel grid optimization [S2], and adaptive sampling strategies [S3][S12, unofficial]. The configuration has become increasingly complex to handle diverse scenarios such as dynamic scenes [S8], blurred images [S11], and few-shot learning [S13, unofficial].
+The concept of training configuration for neural radiance fields originated with the seminal NeRF paper [Mildenhall et al. 2020], which established the use of Adam optimizer with specific learning rates and batch sizes. Subsequent works have refined these configurations to address specific challenges such as sparse views [S2, 7471-pixelnerf.md], dynamic scenes [S11, 17], and efficiency [S6, 13]. The field has evolved to include specialized configurations for differentiable rendering, object detection [S15], and incremental learning [S16].
 
